---

<Article
  title="Cascadia Scoring Cards Explained: All Five Animals"
  description="Bear pairs, elk lines, salmon runs, hawk sight lines and fox neighbours — and which card sets are hardest."
  game="cascadia"
  gameTitle="Cascadia"
  category="Rules"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Cascadia draws one scoring card per animal at setup, and that combination is
    the game. The same five animals reward completely different shapes depending
    on which cards came out, which is why the box stays interesting for dozens of
    plays. Here's what each animal is fundamentally about.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Each animal has a fixed theme — bears want groups, elk want lines, salmon want runs, hawks want isolation, foxes want variety. The card variant only changes the specific sizes and point values." />

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Read the actual cards before you draft">
    Point values differ between variants, sometimes dramatically. The themes below
    hold for every version of each animal, but always check the card in front of
    you for the exact numbers — spending your first two turns studying them is
    never wasted.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>🐻 Bear — Groups</h2>

  <p>
    Bears score for forming <strong>groups of a specific size</strong>, and most
    variants want <strong>pairs</strong>: exactly two bears touching each other,
    with no third bear attached.
  </p>

  <p>
    The trap is obvious once you've been burned by it — adding a third bear to a
    scoring pair can destroy the pair entirely rather than improve it. Bears are
    the animal where you most need to stop placing.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plan for exact sizes.</strong> Leave deliberate gaps so pairs can't accidentally merge.</li>
    <li><strong>Most variants cap out.</strong> There's usually a maximum number of scoring groups, plus a bonus for reaching it.</li>
    <li><strong>Bears are low-volume, high-value.</strong> A handful of correct groups outscores a dozen sloppy ones.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>🦌 Elk — Lines and Formations</h2>

  <p>
    Elk want to be arranged in <strong>shapes</strong> — usually straight lines of
    increasing length, with longer lines worth disproportionately more. Some
    variants reward other formations instead of pure lines.
  </p>

  <p>
    Elk are the most spatially demanding animal in the game. A line of four is
    worth far more than two lines of two, so elk placements have to be planned
    several turns out, and a single tile taken by an opponent can ruin the plan.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Don't start an elk line you can't finish">
    Long lines need a straight run of tiles you don't yet own. If the display
    isn't cooperating by the time you're two elk in, take the points for the
    shorter line and move on rather than holding out for a fourth.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>🐟 Salmon — Runs</h2>

  <p>
    Salmon score for <strong>runs</strong>: chains of adjacent salmon, longer is
    better. The defining restriction is that a salmon in a run may not have too
    many salmon neighbours — the chain has to stay a chain, not become a blob.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practice that means salmon need room to snake across your landscape. If
    you clump them, the run breaks and the whole chain can score nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>🦅 Hawk — Isolation</h2>

  <p>
    Hawks are the opposite of every other animal: they want to be
    <strong>alone</strong>. A hawk generally scores only if it has no other hawk
    adjacent to it. Some variants add line-of-sight scoring, where two isolated
    hawks looking at each other across a straight line of tiles earn extra.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hawks are the friendliest animal for beginners because they need no
    long-term shape — just space. They're also the easiest to over-collect: past
    a certain point you run out of non-adjacent places to put them.
  </p>

  <h2>🦊 Fox — Variety</h2>

  <p>
    Foxes score based on the <strong>different animals adjacent to them</strong>.
    A fox surrounded by a bear, an elk and a salmon is worth far more than one
    surrounded by nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Foxes are the great reconcilers of Cascadia. They reward the messy middle of
    your landscape where several species meet, which means they pair beautifully
    with an otherwise scattered board. Place them last, into whatever variety you
    happened to create.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable title="Quick reference: what each animal wants">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Animal</th>
          <th>Wants</th>
          <th>Difficulty to plan</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>🐻 Bear</td>
          <td>Groups of an exact size, usually pairs</td>
          <td>Medium — knowing when to stop</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🦌 Elk</td>
          <td>Lines and formations, longer is better</td>
          <td><strong>Hardest</strong> — needs turns of setup</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🐟 Salmon</td>
          <td>Chains that don't clump</td>
          <td>Hard — needs open space</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🦅 Hawk</td>
          <td>No adjacent hawks</td>
          <td><strong>Easiest</strong> — just spread out</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>🦊 Fox</td>
          <td>Varied animal neighbours</td>
          <td>Easy — place late, opportunistically</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>Which Card Sets Are Hardest?</h2>

  <p>
    The difficulty of a game comes from how many animals demand
    <em>long-term spatial commitment</em> at once. If the elk and salmon cards
    both want long formations, you're in for a tight, punishing game where
    picking one to abandon early is usually correct.
  </p>

  <p>
    Conversely, a setup where hawks and foxes carry big point values is a
    forgiving, flowing game — both reward reacting to what you're given rather
    than planning.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="How to choose what to chase">
    You cannot maximise all five. Pick two animals to genuinely commit to — ideally
    one that needs space (elk or salmon) and one that's opportunistic (fox or
    hawk) — and treat the other three as bonus points. Trying to score everything
    is the single most reliable way to lose at Cascadia.
  </InfoBox>

  <p>
    For how the animals interact with the terrain half of the scoresheet, see
    <a href="/games/cascadia/cascadia-beginner-strategy/">habitat corridors vs wildlife</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
